From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 13:26:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/35] media: mediatek: camraw: Zero-copy buffers, frame
 timing in the hard IRQ, media device

The replay now runs (correct colour picture of both halves), with two
CQ trigger delay errors per stream: the per-frame lists were started from
the IRQ thread, which also copied the 2.4 MB picture out of uncached
memory at every frame done, so a start of frame could wait longer than
the command queue start period and two starts of frame merged.

- The picture is written straight into the queued V4L2 buffer: the queue
  is dma-contig (MMAP and DMABUF) on the RAW-A device, which shares the
  IOMMU domain with every engine of the frame (checked at probe). At each
  application only the picture address words of the per-frame copy are
  patched (camraw_replay_patch_picture); frames without a queued buffer go
  to the frame request's own picture planes.
- Start of frame and frame done (SW_PASS1_DONE) are handled in the hard
  IRQ: the next application is made at once, and the buffer of the frame
  in flight is completed with the frame number and start of frame time of
  that frame. A buffer whose frame never finished is returned with an
  error when its ring slot comes round again. The IRQ thread keeps the
  logging, snapshots and the first-frame probe report (probe points filled
  in the first queued buffers).
- V4L2_EVENT_FRAME_SYNC at every start of frame, with the frame number the
  completed buffer will carry, for sensor control timing in user space.
- A media device of its own (driver name mtk-camraw-mt6878) with the video
  node as its one entity, so that the node can be found by enumerating
  media devices. The input is named after the sensor interface entity
  that feeds it, to find the sensor in that sensor interface's graph.
- VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES: the one captured size.
- apply_delay_us and reapply=2 are gone (the order of section 11 never
  toggles per frame; the delay made no difference).

